    <h1>Values and Bindings</h1>
    <p class="article-meta" data-pagefind-ignore>
      <span>4 min read</span>
    </p>
    <p>GCSL programs compute values. A block can return one value or construct a
record of named fields, so you can use configuration data inside ordinary
expressions without a separate data format.</p>
<h2 id="blocks-and-records">Blocks and records <a class="heading-anchor" href="#blocks-and-records" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Newlines or semicolons separate items inside a file or <code>{ ... }</code> block.
A block without fields returns its final expression, or an explicit
<code>= expression</code> result. A block with fields returns their record instead;
it cannot also have an explicit result. The empty record <code>{}</code> represents
an operation with no useful result.</p>
<p>A field is a public part of the resulting record. A <code>let</code> binding is local
to its scope. This block keeps the intermediate name private:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let release = "0.1.0"
name = "gcsl"
archive = "{name}-{release}.tar.gz"
</code></pre></div>
<p>Top-level fields and named functions can refer to declarations written
later. Inner blocks evaluate their ordinary fields in order. Private
<code>let</code> bindings become available after their declaration.</p>
<h2 id="updating-a-binding">Updating a binding <a class="heading-anchor" href="#updating-a-binding" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>:=</code> replaces the value associated with a local name. For an ordinary
<code>let</code>, this is restricted to the block that declares it. Use <code>let mut</code>
when an inner block of the same function needs to update the variable:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let mut total = 0
for cost in [3, 8, 5] {
    total := total + cost
}
total
</code></pre></div>
<p>The variable keeps its declared or inferred type. Values themselves do not
change: replacing a record field or list element constructs an updated
value for the binding. Other bindings and previously created closures
keep the values they already held.</p>
<p>A closure captures values when it is created. It cannot update a mutable
variable belonging to its enclosing function. Pass in a value and return
an updated value when you need that communication.</p>
<h2 id="names-and-arithmetic">Names and arithmetic <a class="heading-anchor" href="#names-and-arithmetic" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Names can contain hyphens. Write spaces around subtraction so that
<code>left - right</code> is an operation rather than a name. Integer arithmetic uses
signed 64-bit values with checked overflow. Floating-point values use
64-bit precision. <code>/</code> produces a floating-point result; <code>//</code> and <code>%</code> are
integer floor division and modulo.</p>
<p>Integer literals adapt to an expected numeric type. A bound <code>Int</code> does
not silently become a <code>Float</code>; use <code>float(value)</code> when that conversion is
intended. Conditions require <code>Bool</code>, not a nonzero integer or a nonempty
string. See <code>gcsl(5)</code> for syntax and operator rules.</p>
<h2 id="durations-and-sizes">Durations and sizes <a class="heading-anchor" href="#durations-and-sizes" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>Duration</code> and <code>Size</code> hold amounts with units. Write a duration as a
number followed by units from largest to smallest: <code>30s</code>, <code>500ms</code>,
<code>1h30m</code>, <code>2d</code>. The units are <code>d</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>m</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>ms</code>, <code>us</code> and <code>ns</code>, and a
day is exactly 24 hours. Write a size with one unit: <code>512B</code>, the decimal
<code>KB</code> to <code>PB</code>, or the binary <code>KiB</code> to <code>PiB</code>, as in <code>2GiB</code>. The last part of
a literal can have a fraction if the result is a whole number of
nanoseconds or bytes, so <code>1.5s</code> and <code>0.5KiB</code> are allowed but <code>1.5ns</code> is
not. Units are case-sensitive.</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let timeout = 1m30s
retry_after = timeout // 3
budget = 2 * timeout
memory = 2GiB + 512MiB
long = timeout &gt; 1m
message = "giving up after {timeout}"
</code></pre></div>
<p>Add or subtract two values of the same kind, multiply one by an <code>Int</code>,
and use <code>//</code> to divide by an <code>Int</code> or by another value of the same kind.
Dividing by the same kind gives an <code>Int</code>. <code>%</code> gives the remainder of one
value by another of the same kind. Values of the same kind can be
compared. Durations and sizes never mix with each other, with <code>Int</code> or
with <code>Float</code>, and an integer is never a duration, so write <code>0s</code> rather
than <code>0</code>. <code>/</code> is not available, because the result would not be a whole
number of the smallest unit.</p>
<p>A value is shown as its simplest literal: <code>retry_after</code> above is <code>30s</code>,
<code>budget</code> is <code>3m</code>, and <code>memory</code> is <code>2560MiB</code>.</p>

